tion spending and Republican defection ads", "Talarico's fundraising dominance ($68.6M raised, $21.5M on hand vs. Paxton's $1.8M)", "Texas's Republican lean (R+6 baseline) limits how far Democrats can run ahead"], "rationale": "Polling averages put Talarico up about three points, prediction markets price his win chances near 60 percent, and raters split between Tossup and Lean D, so a modest Democratic edge is warranted. Paxton's weak finances, heavy outside opposition, and intra-party opposition reinforce the tilt, though Texas's Republican fundamentals keep this short of Likely D.", "watch": "A late shift among skeptical Republicans or a turnout differential in Texas's metro counties could move the race either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: John Cornyn (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Texas's open Senate seat has become one of the closest races of 2026, with state Senator James Talarico facing Attorney General Ken Paxton after Paxton ousted incumbent John Cornyn in the primary. Recent polls from mid-September consistently show Talarico leading by one to six points, with the polling average around three points. Prediction markets give Talarico roughly a 58 to 62 percent chance, and expert raters split between Tossup and Lean D.\n\nTalarico holds a large financial advantage, with $21.5 million on hand compared with under $2 million for Paxton, who also faces roughly $27 million in outside opposition spending and ads from Republicans refusing to back him. Texas's Republican-leaning fundamentals temper the Democratic edge, leaving a narrow but real Talarico advantage heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6697, "margin": 2.08}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6924, "margin": 2.5}]}